## The finding

`crime_00010` — **Sync I/O in Hot Path**, `src/api/files.ts:12–15`, confidence 0.90.

Summary, verbatim:

> "3 synchronous I/O calls inside domain function — each invocation blocks the event loop for the duration of the I/O. Async counterparts exist for every method captured."

Evidence, verbatim:

> - "`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"
> - "lines: 12, 13, 15"
> - "swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it"

## The blocking calls

Three, all in `loadPlanSchema`, all from `node:fs`:

| Line | Call | Cost per request |
|---|---|---|
| 12 | `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | one blocking `stat(2)` |
| 13 | `statSync(SCHEMA_PATH)` | a second blocking `stat(2)` on the same path |
| 15 | `readFileSync(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8")` | blocking `open`/`read`/`close` |

That's three synchronous syscall round-trips on every call, and they run on the main thread — not the libuv threadpool. Under concurrency they don't interleave: request N+1 can't even be *dequeued* until request N's `read` returns. That is exactly the shape that produces event-loop lag plus a p99 blowout while p50 still looks fine, because the queue only builds once arrival rate approaches the serialized service rate. `JSON.parse` on line 15 is also synchronous and scales with file size, but it isn't I/O — fix the syscalls first and re-measure.

Two secondary notes from reading the file: `existsSync` + `statSync` + `readFileSync` is a TOCTOU race (the file can vanish between L12 and L15, and `readFileSync` will throw where the guard implied a `null`), and `SCHEMA_PATH` is `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` — `crime_00019` in the same scan. Once this is async, the missing-file path stops being a special case, which fixes the race for free.

## Smallest async migration

Nothing in the fixture imports `loadPlanSchema` (I grepped — it's exported but has no in-repo callers), so the blast radius is whatever consumes it downstream.

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  let text: string;
  try {
    text = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "ENOENT") return null;
    throw err;
  }
  if (text.length === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(text);
}
```

What this preserves: the name, the empty parameter list, and all three observable outcomes — missing file → `null`, empty file → `null`, otherwise the parsed schema. A zero-byte file and an empty utf8 string are the same condition, so the `stat.size === 0` check maps one-to-one onto `text.length === 0`.

What it necessarily changes: the return type becomes `Promise<unknown>`. There is no way around that — a function cannot do non-blocking I/O and still return its value synchronously. Callers add one `await`; the signature is otherwise identical. If a caller genuinely cannot be made async, the honest fix is to hoist the load to startup and pass the schema in, not to keep the sync call.

The three syscalls also collapse into one. `existsSync`/`statSync` were only there to decide whether to read, and `readFile` already answers both questions — through `ENOENT` and through the returned length. So the migration is strictly less I/O per call, not just non-blocking I/O.

## Why not caching

A module-level cache would flatten the p99 graph without touching the defect. It also fails in the specific case that matters here: a cold process under load still eats the full blocking read on request one, and with concurrent arrivals every in-flight request queues behind it — which is the incident you're describing, not a steady-state one. It additionally freezes the schema for the process lifetime, turning a config change into a deploy. Cache later if profiling says the read is hot; it's an optimization on top of a correct async call, not a substitute for one.

## One caveat on the scan itself

The ranking has this at `severity: low` (0.4), tenth of 42, below three orphaned-route findings by aggregate risk. For a per-request blocking call in something named `src/api/`, that's under-ranked — the detector's own `tier: "domain"` classification and 0.9 confidence both say it knew what it was looking at. Worth a look if you're tuning `sync_io_in_hotpath` severity; the evidence block is doing its job, the score isn't.
